Count the frequency of each character, then check that every character that appears has the same count.

The examples show a trailing space in the input (`"abacbc "`), which is probably a formatting artifact. The constraints say `s` is lowercase letters only. If the space were really part of the string, it would count as a character with frequency 1 and break the first example, so I count only characters in `'a'..'z'`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool areOccurrencesEqual(string s) {
        int cnt[26] = {0};
        for (char c : s) {
            if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') cnt[c - 'a']++;
        }
        int f = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
            if (cnt[i] == 0) continue;
            if (f == 0) f = cnt[i];
            else if (f != cnt[i]) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }
};
```

