traces.inference_trace:
Sort the array, then let `dp[i]` be the number of trees whose root is `arr[i]`. Every number alone is a one-node tree, so `dp[i]` starts at 1.

For each `i`, look at every `j < i` with `arr[i] % arr[j] == 0`. Let `q = arr[i] / arr[j]`. If `q` is in the array, at index `k`, then `arr[j]` and `arr[k]` can be the left and right children, which adds `dp[j] * dp[k]` to `dp[i]`. Iterating over all `j` counts the ordered pairs (left, right) naturally, so the swapped arrangement is picked up when `j` reaches the other factor.

A hash map from value to index (or to `dp`) gives the lookup for `q`. The answer is the sum of all `dp[i]`, taken modulo 10^9 + 7. Products of two `dp` values need `long long`.

The loop is O(n²), which is fine for n ≤ 1000.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int numFactoredBinaryTrees(vector<int>& arr) {
        const long long MOD = 1000000007LL;
        sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());
        int n = arr.size();
        unordered_map<long long, long long> dp;
        long long ans = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            long long cur = 1;
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (arr[i] % arr[j] == 0) {
                    long long q = arr[i] / arr[j];
                    auto it = dp.find(q);
                    if (it != dp.end()) {
                        cur = (cur + dp[arr[j]] * it->second) % MOD;
                    }
                }
            }
            dp[arr[i]] = cur;
            ans = (ans + cur) % MOD;
        }
        return (int)ans;
    }
};
```

