Scan the string from the end, collecting alphanumeric characters in uppercase. After every `k` characters, insert a dash before the next one. Then reverse the result.

Details:
- Keep a counter `cnt` of characters added since the last dash.
- For each non-dash character from the end: if `cnt == k`, push `'-'` and reset `cnt` to 0. Then push the uppercased character and increment `cnt`.
- Because the dash is only pushed when another character follows, there is no trailing dash at the front after reversing.
- If the string has no alphanumeric characters, the result is empty.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    string licenseKeyFormatting(string s, int k) {
        string r;
        int cnt = 0;
        for (int i = (int)s.size() - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
            char c = s[i];
            if (c == '-') continue;
            if (cnt == k) {
                r.push_back('-');
                cnt = 0;
            }
            r.push_back(toupper(c));
            cnt++;
        }
        reverse(r.begin(), r.end());
        return r;
    }
};
```

