Use patience sorting: keep a `tails` array where `tails[i]` is the smallest possible tail of a strictly increasing subsequence of length `i+1`. For each number, `lower_bound` finds the first tail that is at least as large. Replace that tail with the number, or append it if no such tail exists. Using `lower_bound` (not `upper_bound`) keeps the subsequence strictly increasing. The answer is the size of `tails`. This runs in O(n log n).

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int lengthOfLIS(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> tails;
        for (int x : nums) {
            auto it = lower_bound(tails.begin(), tails.end(), x);
            if (it == tails.end()) tails.push_back(x);
            else *it = x;
        }
        return tails.size();
    }
};
```

