Sort the words by length, then run a DP over a hash map from word to the longest chain ending at that word. For each word, try deleting each character. If the shorter word is in the map, the chain ending here can be one longer than the chain ending there. The answer is the maximum value over all words.

The examples in the statement contain trailing spaces inside the strings, but those are formatting artifacts. The same logic would work on them anyway, since the trailing space is just another character.

Duplicate words are not an issue: the same word gets the same value, and deleting a character gives a strictly shorter word.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int longestStrChain(vector<string>& words) {
        sort(words.begin(), words.end(), [](const string& a, const string& b) {
            return a.size() < b.size();
        });
        unordered_map<string, int> dp;
        int best = 0;
        for (auto& w : words) {
            int cur = 1;
            for (size_t i = 0; i < w.size(); i++) {
                string p = w.substr(0, i) + w.substr(i + 1);
                auto it = dp.find(p);
                if (it != dp.end()) cur = max(cur, it->second + 1);
            }
            dp[w] = max(dp[w], cur);
            best = max(best, cur);
        }
        return best;
    }
};
```

