Use memoized recursion: `f(n) = 1 + min(n%2 + f(n/2), n%3 + f(n/3))`, with `f(0) = 0` and `f(1) = 1`.

The reasoning is that eating single oranges is only useful to reach a number divisible by 2 or 3. To use the halving move, eat `n%2` oranges first, then halve. To use the thirding move, eat `n%3` oranges first, then take `2*(n/3)`, which leaves `n/3`. Each branch costs the single-orange days plus one day for the move. The number of distinct states is O(log² n), so a hash map for memoization is enough.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    unordered_map<int,int> memo;
    int minDays(int n) {
        if (n <= 1) return n;
        auto it = memo.find(n);
        if (it != memo.end()) return it->second;
        int r = 1 + min(n % 2 + minDays(n / 2), n % 3 + minDays(n / 3));
        memo[n] = r;
        return r;
    }
};
```

