Use prefix sums and try both orderings: the first-length subarray before the second-length one, and the reverse.

Let `P` be the prefix sums, so the sum of the window ending at index `i` (exclusive) with length `L` is `P[i] - P[i-L]`.

The helper `f(L, M)` returns the best sum of an L-window that lies entirely before an M-window:
- Iterate `i` from `L+M` to `n`, where `i` is the exclusive end of the M-window.
- Before handling each `i`, update `bestL` with the L-window ending at `i-M`, i.e. `P[i-M] - P[i-M-L]`.
- Compute the candidate `bestL + P[i] - P[i-M]` and keep the maximum.

The answer is `max(f(firstLen, secondLen), f(secondLen, firstLen))`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int maxSumTwoNoOverlap(vector<int>& nums, int firstLen, int secondLen) {
        int n = nums.size();
        vector<int> P(n + 1, 0);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) P[i + 1] = P[i] + nums[i];
        auto f = [&](int L, int M) {
            int bestL = 0, res = 0;
            for (int i = L + M; i <= n; i++) {
                bestL = max(bestL, P[i - M] - P[i - M - L]);
                res = max(res, bestL + P[i] - P[i - M]);
            }
            return res;
        };
        return max(f(firstLen, secondLen), f(secondLen, firstLen));
    }
};
```

