Binary search on the number of days. For a given day `d`, scan the garden and count how many bouquets of `k` adjacent bloomed flowers can be made. If that count is at least `m`, then `d` days is enough.

First check feasibility: if `m * k > n`, return -1. Use `long long` for this product, since `m` can reach 10^6 and `k` can reach 10^5.

The search range is from the minimum to the maximum value in `bloomDay`. Feasibility is monotonic in `d`, so I find the smallest feasible `d`.

To count bouquets for a given `d`, keep a running count of consecutive flowers with `bloomDay[i] <= d`. Each time the run reaches `k`, add one bouquet and reset the run to 0. If a flower has not bloomed, reset the run to 0.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int minDays(vector<int>& bloomDay, int m, int k) {
        long long n = bloomDay.size();
        if ((long long)m * k > n) return -1;
        int lo = *min_element(bloomDay.begin(), bloomDay.end());
        int hi = *max_element(bloomDay.begin(), bloomDay.end());
        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            long long cnt = 0;
            int run = 0;
            for (int x : bloomDay) {
                if (x <= mid) {
                    run++;
                    if (run == k) {
                        cnt++;
                        run = 0;
                    }
                } else {
                    run = 0;
                }
            }
            if (cnt >= m) hi = mid;
            else lo = mid + 1;
        }
        return lo;
    }
};
```

