This is a standard DP problem. Let `dp[i][j]` be true when the first `i` characters of `s` match the first `j` characters of `p`.

- Base case: `dp[0][0] = true`.
- If `p[j-1] == '*'`, there are two options:
  - Use zero copies of the preceding element: `dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-2]`.
  - Use one more copy, which requires `i > 0` and that `p[j-2]` matches `s[i-1]` (equal characters or `'.'`): `dp[i][j] |= dp[i-1][j]`.
- Otherwise, `dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1]` when `i > 0` and `p[j-1]` matches `s[i-1]`.

For `j` starting at 1, a `'*'` always has a preceding character, so `j-2 >= 0` is safe.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isMatch(string s, string p) {
        int m = s.size(), n = p.size();
        vector<vector<bool>> dp(m + 1, vector<bool>(n + 1, false));
        dp[0][0] = true;
        for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (p[j-1] == '*') {
                    if (j >= 2) {
                        dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-2];
                        if (i > 0 && (p[j-2] == '.' || p[j-2] == s[i-1]))
                            dp[i][j] = dp[i][j] || dp[i-1][j];
                    }
                } else {
                    if (i > 0 && (p[j-1] == '.' || p[j-1] == s[i-1]))
                        dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1];
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[m][n];
    }
};
```

