Since n ≤ 10^6, the answer is at most 1224444, so a brute-force search works: start at n+1 and test each number for balance. The gaps between balanced numbers are small, and the check is cheap.

The check counts the digits of x. A number is balanced if no digit is 0 and every digit d that appears has a count of exactly d.

The smallest balanced number above 10^6 is 1224444, which is 7 digits (1 once, 2 twice, 4 four times). So the loop ends by about 1.2 million iterations at worst, which is fast enough.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int nextBeautifulNumber(int n) {
        for (int x = n + 1;; x++) {
            int cnt[10] = {0};
            int y = x;
            while (y) {
                cnt[y % 10]++;
                y /= 10;
            }
            bool ok = cnt[0] == 0;
            for (int d = 1; d < 10 && ok; d++) {
                if (cnt[d] != 0 && cnt[d] != d) ok = false;
            }
            if (ok) return x;
        }
    }
};
```

