traces.inference_trace:
I'll use a bottom-up DP: `dp[len][i][j]` is true if `s1.substr(i, len)` can be scrambled into `s2.substr(j, len)`.

**Base case:** for `len = 1`, `dp[1][i][j]` is true when `s1[i] == s2[j]`.

**Transition:** for `len` from 2 up to `n`, try every split `k` from 1 to `len-1`.
- No swap: `dp[k][i][j] && dp[len-k][i+k][j+k]`.
- Swap: `dp[k][i][j+len-k] && dp[len-k][i+k][j]`.

The answer is `dp[n][0][0]`.

The table has size 31×30×30. Each of the O(n³) states loops over up to n splits, so the total work is O(n⁴), about 810k operations for n = 30.

The problem's examples contain trailing spaces, but the actual inputs are lowercase letters, and the DP works for any characters either way.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isScramble(string s1, string s2) {
        int n = s1.size();
        if (s2.size() != (size_t)n) return false;
        vector<vector<vector<char>>> dp(n + 1, vector<vector<char>>(n, vector<char>(n, 0)));
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++)
                dp[1][i][j] = (s1[i] == s2[j]);
        for (int len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
            for (int i = 0; i + len <= n; i++) {
                for (int j = 0; j + len <= n; j++) {
                    bool ok = false;
                    for (int k = 1; k < len && !ok; k++) {
                        if (dp[k][i][j] && dp[len - k][i + k][j + k]) ok = true;
                        else if (dp[k][i][j + len - k] && dp[len - k][i + k][j]) ok = true;
                    }
                    dp[len][i][j] = ok;
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[n][0][0];
    }
};
```

