Approach: bitmask DP over subsets of the array. If the total sum is not divisible by k, or the largest element exceeds the target (sum / k), return false.

Let `dp[mask]` be the sum of the elements in `mask` modulo the target, or -1 if `mask` is unreachable. Start with `dp[0] = 0`. For each reachable mask and each unused element `i`, if `dp[mask] + nums[i] <= target`, then set `dp[mask | 1<<i] = (dp[mask] + nums[i]) % target`. The answer is whether `dp[full] == 0`.

This works because we fill the groups one at a time. The current partial group has sum `dp[mask]`, and an element is only added if it fits within the target. Since all elements are positive and the total equals `k * target`, reaching the full mask means every group was completed exactly.

When the sum is 0 the target would be 0, but elements are at least 1, so this cannot happen.

The cost is 2^16 · 16 ≈ 1M operations, which is fine.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool canPartitionKSubsets(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        int n = nums.size();
        int sum = 0, mx = 0;
        for (int x : nums) {
            sum += x;
            mx = max(mx, x);
        }
        if (sum % k != 0) return false;
        int target = sum / k;
        if (mx > target) return false;
        vector<int> dp(1 << n, -1);
        dp[0] = 0;
        for (int mask = 0; mask < (1 << n); mask++) {
            if (dp[mask] < 0) continue;
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                if (mask & (1 << i)) continue;
                int nm = mask | (1 << i);
                if (dp[nm] >= 0) continue;
                if (dp[mask] + nums[i] <= target) {
                    dp[nm] = (dp[mask] + nums[i]) % target;
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[(1 << n) - 1] == 0;
    }
};
```

