The examples contain trailing spaces inside the strings (e.g. "with "). The real constraints say lowercase letters only, so I'll ignore any non-lowercase characters in stickers. The target's spaces can't be ignored in the same way, so I'll treat them as ordinary characters. A space in the target would only be coverable by a sticker containing a space, so I'll count all characters generically using 128-sized count arrays. This handles both cases correctly.

Transition: for each reachable `mask`, find the first unset bit `i` in the target. Some sticker must cover that character, so only try stickers containing `target[i]`. For each such sticker, copy its character counts, then walk through the target positions `j` not in `mask`. If the sticker still has `target[j]`, decrement its count and set bit `j` in the new mask. Then update `dp[newmask] = min(dp[newmask], dp[mask] + 1)`.

Always fixing the first unset bit is valid because any solution must cover that bit with some sticker, and the order of stickers doesn't matter. This keeps the branching small. Since `newmask` is strictly greater than `mask` (it includes bit `i`), iterating masks in increasing order works.

The answer is `dp[full]`, or -1 if it is unreachable.

Complexity: 2^15 masks × 50 stickers × (15 + 128 for the copy). That is about 2.3×10^8 in the worst case, but pruning to stickers containing the needed character and skipping unreachable masks reduces it. To keep the copy cheap, I'll use an `int[128]` array copy only for stickers that contain the needed character.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int minStickers(vector<string>& stickers, string target) {
        int n = stickers.size();
        vector<array<int,128>> cnt(n);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            cnt[i].fill(0);
            for (char c : stickers[i]) cnt[i][(int)c]++;
        }
        int m = target.size();
        int full = (1 << m) - 1;
        const int INF = 1e9;
        vector<int> dp(full + 1, INF);
        dp[0] = 0;
        for (int mask = 0; mask < full; mask++) {
            if (dp[mask] >= INF) continue;
            int i = 0;
            while (mask >> i & 1) i++;
            char need = target[i];
            for (int s = 0; s < n; s++) {
                if (cnt[s][(int)need] == 0) continue;
                int c[128];
                for (int k = 0; k < 128; k++) c[k] = cnt[s][k];
                int nm = mask;
                for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
                    if (nm >> j & 1) continue;
                    if (c[(int)target[j]] > 0) {
                        c[(int)target[j]]--;
                        nm |= 1 << j;
                    }
                }
                if (dp[mask] + 1 < dp[nm]) dp[nm] = dp[mask] + 1;
            }
        }
        return dp[full] >= INF ? -1 : dp[full];
    }
};
```

