traces.inference_trace:
Sort the products, then for each prefix length binary-search for the first product that is >= the prefix. The next three products from that position are the candidates, and each is kept only if it starts with the prefix.

Since the prefix only grows, the lower bound never moves backward, so the search can start from the previous position.

Steps:
1. Sort `products`.
2. Keep `start = 0` and build `prefix` one character at a time.
3. For each prefix, set `start` to `lower_bound(products.begin() + start, products.end(), prefix) - products.begin()`.
4. For `i` in `start .. start+2` within bounds, check that `products[i]` starts with `prefix` using `compare(0, prefix.size(), prefix) == 0`. If it does, add it to the result, otherwise stop.

The cost is about 1000 prefixes × (log 1000 comparisons + 3 checks) × up to 1000 characters per comparison, which is fine.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<string>> suggestedProducts(vector<string>& products, string searchWord) {
        sort(products.begin(), products.end());
        vector<vector<string>> res;
        string prefix;
        int start = 0;
        int n = products.size();
        for (char c : searchWord) {
            prefix += c;
            start = lower_bound(products.begin() + start, products.end(), prefix) - products.begin();
            vector<string> cur;
            for (int i = start; i < n && i < start + 3; i++) {
                if (products[i].compare(0, prefix.size(), prefix) == 0)
                    cur.push_back(products[i]);
                else
                    break;
            }
            res.push_back(cur);
        }
        return res;
    }
};
```

