Recursion depth could reach 1e5, so I use an iterative DFS to avoid stack overflow.

Iterative DFS design:
- Store `parent`, `depth`, and an adjacency list.
- Keep an explicit stack of (node, state). On entry, compute the answer for the node and push it onto `stk[nums[node]]`. Then iterate through its children using an index pointer `idx[node]`. When all children are done, pop it from `stk[nums[node]]`.

Precompute `cop[i][j]` = (gcd(i, j) == 1).

Implementation:
- `st` is a vector<int> of nodes. Push 0 with depth 0 and `parent` -1.
- Loop while `st` is not empty. Let `u = st.back()`. If `idx[u] == 0` and not yet entered, enter: compute the answer, then push onto the value stack. I use a separate `entered` flag array.
- Then advance: while `idx[u] < adj[u].size()`, take `v = adj[u][idx[u]++]`. If `v != parent[u]`, set `parent[v] = u`, `depth[v] = depth[u] + 1`, push `v`, and break out to continue the main loop. If no child was pushed, exit: pop `stk[nums[u]]` and pop `st`.

Computing the answer for u: for v in 1..50, if `cop[nums[u]][v]` and `stk[v]` is non-empty, take `stk[v].back()` as a candidate and keep the one with max depth; the answer is -1 if none.

