Use a greedy approach: at each step, choose the letter with more remaining, unless the last two characters are already that letter. In that case, choose the other letter.

At each step:
- If the last two characters are both 'a', we must place 'b'.
- Else if the last two characters are both 'b', we must place 'a'.
- Otherwise, place the letter with more remaining.

Because a valid string is guaranteed to exist, this greedy never gets stuck. When forced to place a letter, its count is nonzero, since otherwise no solution would exist.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    string strWithout3a3b(int a, int b) {
        string s;
        while (a > 0 || b > 0) {
            int n = s.size();
            bool placeA;
            if (n >= 2 && s[n-1] == 'a' && s[n-2] == 'a') placeA = false;
            else if (n >= 2 && s[n-1] == 'b' && s[n-2] == 'b') placeA = true;
            else placeA = a >= b;
            if (placeA) {
                s += 'a';
                a--;
            } else {
                s += 'b';
                b--;
            }
        }
        return s;
    }
};
```

