traces.inference_trace:
This uses the standard greedy two-pointer approach with backtracking to the last `*`.

1. Walk through `s` with index `i` and through `p` with index `j`. Remember the position of the most recent `*` in `star`, and the position in `s` where it started matching in `mark`.
2. If `p[j]` is `?` or equals `s[i]`, advance both indices.
3. If `p[j]` is `*`, record `star = j` and `mark = i`, then advance only `j`. This first tries to match the star with the empty sequence.
4. Otherwise, if a previous star exists, backtrack: set `j = star + 1`, increment `mark`, and set `i = mark`. This makes the star absorb one more character of `s`.
5. If there is no mismatch handler (no earlier star), return false.
6. After `s` is consumed, skip any remaining `*` in `p`. The match succeeds only if `j` has reached the end of `p`.

Each backtrack increases `mark`, so the loop runs in O(n·m) time in the worst case with O(1) extra space.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isMatch(string s, string p) {
        int n = s.size(), m = p.size();
        int i = 0, j = 0, star = -1, mark = 0;
        while (i < n) {
            if (j < m && (p[j] == '?' || p[j] == s[i])) {
                i++;
                j++;
            } else if (j < m && p[j] == '*') {
                star = j;
                mark = i;
                j++;
            } else if (star != -1) {
                j = star + 1;
                mark++;
                i = mark;
            } else {
                return false;
            }
        }
        while (j < m && p[j] == '*') j++;
        return j == m;
    }
};
```

